
<p>I first recommended Strategery <a href="https://daringfireball.net/linked/2009/05/04/strategery">back in May 2009</a>, including a link to <a href="https://www.macworld.com/article/196162/strategery.html">Friedman’s review at Macworld</a>. I described it thus:</p>

<blockquote>
  <p>Strategery is a beautiful $2 Risk-like strategy game for the
iPhone. Lots of fun and the gameplay has a great feel.</p>
</blockquote>

<p>Still lots of fun and still has a great feel. <a href="https://strategerygame.com/app">It now costs $5</a>, but, if you’re an existing user — even if you only paid $2 back when I told you to 17 years ago — it’s a free update. And it’s a great story: the original Strategery game <a href="https://mastodon.social/@jimray/117138792023999673">finally gave up the compatibility ghost with iOS 27</a>. Friedman made his own version of the game, including multiplayer support, but before releasing it on his own under a new name, he reached out to the original developers and it turns out everyone was happy to make a deal. Sometimes all you need to do is ask.</p>

<p>(First thing you should do after installing the new version is go into settings and turn on “Classic Hex Edges”. The default style — Lex edges? — doesn’t look right.)</p>

